Approach: Product of Array Except Self
The main idea is to calculate the product of all elements to the left of each index and multiply it by the product of all elements to the right.

For every index i:

answer[i] = product of elements before i × product of elements after i

We do this in two passes without using division.

1. Prefix Product — Left to Right
We maintain a variable pre that stores the product of all elements before the current index.

Initially:

pre = 1;

For every i:

res[i] = pre;
pre *= nums[i];

Example:

nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]

After first pass:

res = [1, 1, 2, 6]

Why?

index 0 → nothing on left → 1
index 1 → 1           → 1
index 2 → 1 × 2       → 2
index 3 → 1 × 2 × 3   → 6

So now res[i] contains the prefix product.

2. Suffix Product — Right to Left
Now we need the product of all elements after i.

Maintain another variable:

suf = 1;

Traverse from right to left:

res[i] *= suf;
suf *= nums[i];

Using:

nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]

Before second pass:
res = [1, 1, 2, 6]

Process from right:

i = 3
res[3] = 6 × 1 = 6
suf = 4

i = 2
res[2] = 2 × 4 = 8
suf = 12

i = 1
res[1] = 1 × 12 = 12
suf = 24

i = 0
res[0] = 1 × 24 = 24

Final:

res = [24, 12, 8, 6]

3. Why this works
At the end:

res[i] = prefix product × suffix product

which is exactly:

product of everything except nums[i]

For example, at index 2:

nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]

left product  = 1 × 2 = 2
right product = 4

answer[2] = 2 × 4 = 8

Why no division?
A common approach is:

total product / nums[i]

But that fails when the array contains 0.

This two-pass prefix/suffix approach handles zeros naturally.

Example:

nums = [1, 2, 0, 4]

answer = [0, 0, 8, 0]

No special zero handling is required.

Complexity
First loop → O(n)
Second loop → O(n)
Total → O(n)
Result array → O(n)
Extra variables pre and suf → O(1)
So:

Time Complexity:  O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)


CODE-
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> productExceptSelf(vector<int>& nums) {

        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> res(n);

        int pre = 1;
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
            res[i] = pre;
            pre *= nums[i];
        }

        int suf = 1;
        for(int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--){
            res[i] *= suf;
            suf *= nums[i];
        }

        return res;
    }
};
